{"schema":2,"commit":"8242797ece90786c57d08aad8d6ae84eaea74d2a","branch":"master","base_sha":"e9bfa1217bcc09f8196f1b17ac2609c46293f2e2","message":"Replace the Express service with a Next.js app on Vercel\n\nThe standalone service in server/ was never deployed anywhere, and keeping it\nalongside a Vercel app would mean maintaining two implementations of the same\nvalidation rules. It is deleted; web/ is the only ingest path now. Its routes\nlive at /v1/... rather than under /api, so the Android client's URL building is\nunchanged and CONSUMPTION_API_URL stays a bare origin.\n\nCredentials are split in two. INGEST_TOKEN is baked into the published APK and\nmust be assumed extractable — anyone can unzip an APK and read its strings — so\nit opens the PUT endpoints and nothing else. Reading the history needs the\ndashboard password, which is never compiled into anything and reaches the\nbrowser as an HMAC-signed httpOnly cookie. The MongoDB URI stays a server-side\nenvironment variable; the car has never held one and still does not.\n\nThe dashboard is a phone-shaped reader for the same data: totals for energy\ntaken and paid, a daily energy chart, an efficiency trend, and every DC session\nwith its power/SOC curve. It reuses the head unit's OLED palette so the two read\nas one app. Every page is a server component and the charts are hand-rolled SVG,\nso the whole thing renders without shipping client JavaScript.\n\nMongoClient is cached on globalThis: a fresh client per request means a fresh\npool per request, and Atlas starts refusing connections long before the traffic\nwould justify it. Index creation moved to the first write of each warm lambda,\nsince a serverless deployment has no boot step to hang it on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","totals":{"added_lines":1975,"ai_added_lines":1955,"human_added_lines":20,"deleted_lines":279,"ai_deleted_lines":0,"human_deleted_lines":279,"files":38,"models":{"claude-opus-5":1955